  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nBroad upper-level ridging will remain the dominant feature through \nmuch of the upcoming work week, maintaining hot temperatures across \nNorth Central and Southwest Montana. Persistent southwesterly flow \non the western periphery of the ridge will also continue to assist \nin the transporting of wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest \ninto the region through at least Tuesday, resulting in degraded air \nquality and reduced visibility especially across the Plains and \nNorthern Rockies. Afternoon highs will gradually warm through the \nperiod, with the hottest conditions expected the late week. Weak \ndisturbances embedded within the southwesterly flow will \nperiodically move across the Northern Rockies. Aided by monsoonal \nmoisture streaming northward, weak shortwaves moving through the \nsouthwesterly flow will support isolated evening convection each \nday. Tuesday and Wednesday will feature slightly increased chances \nas a pair of more pronounced disturbances move through the region. \nConvection will remain primarily terrain focused, with the highest \nprobabilities across Southwestern Montana. Forecast soundings \ncontinue to depict inverted-V profiles, supporting gusty and erratic \noutflow winds as the primary hazard with any stronger thunderstorm. \nFriday into Saturday, the upper-level ridge axis briefly extends \nnorthward across the Rockies, supporting another round of warming \nand pushing temperatures back up towards the 100 degree mark across \nmuch of the plains and valleys.\n\nLooking towards the latter half of the weekend into early next week, \nensembles guidance continues to suggest a transition towards a less \namplified pattern as toughing moves into Northwestern CONUS. As \ntoughing progresses eastward, the ridge begins to weaken and is \nsuppressed southward, supporting a modest cooling trend and breezier \nconditions across the region. However, ensemble guidance continues \nto indicate limited moisture accompanying the weakening trough and \nsubsequent toughing, resulting in low confidence for any meaningful \nincrease in precipitation.\n\n-JR\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nContinued Hot Temperatures...\n\nWildfire smoke remains the primary forecast challenge through at \nleast Tuesday. High resolution modeling continues to depict elevated \nsmoke concentrations across much of the region with the greatest \nimpacts to air quality expect across the Northern Rockies and \nPlains. Confidence is high that wildfire smoke will continue to \nimpact the region, though confidence in the placement and density of \nthe thickest smoke remains lower. As a result, continued adjustments \nto forecast highs may be needed should smoke trends differ from \ncurrent guidance.\n\nConfidence is high that Friday into Saturday will bring the hottest \ntemperatures of the forecast period as upper-level ridging briefly \namplifies over the Northern Rockies. NBM Probabilities indicate a 30 \nto 50 percent chance of reaching 100 degrees across much of the \nplains Friday before increasing to 60 to over 90 percent by \nSaturday. Valley locations are less likely to reach that century \nmark Friday, though probabilities increase into the 40 to 50 percent \nrange by Saturday. The primary source of uncertainty remains the \nextent and persistence of wildfire smoke, as thicker smoke could \nlimit daytime heating and result in muted afternoon highs. \n\nWeakening Ridge, Cooling, and Moisture\u2026\n\nConfidence decreases towards the end of next weekend as ensemble \nguidance begins to diverge on the evolution of the upper-level \npattern. While most ensemble members favor a transition towards a \nless amplified pattern, uncertainty remains regarding the timing of \nthe change and any associated increase in precipitation. \n\n-JR",
